(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-7-sleep-apnea-ohs-vignettes"]={"title":"Obstructive Sleep Apnea and Obesity Hypoventilation Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 7","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 7","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 34-year-old man on a mountain trek at high altitude wakes frequently at night. His tent partner notices that his breathing speeds up and then stops, over and over, without snoring. He has no heart failure and takes no medications. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Panic attacks","Nocturnal asthma","Central sleep apnea","Obstructive sleep apnea"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. High altitude is a risk factor for central sleep apnea, in which increased sensitivity to carbon dioxide produces an unstable pattern of hyperventilation alternating with apnea. Frequent awakenings without snoring fit, since snoring is less common than in obstructive sleep apnea. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 24, 25, 10"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman is diagnosed with obesity hypoventilation syndrome. She has coexisting obstructive sleep apnea, and her awake room-air oxygen saturation is 85% (normal 95-100%). Which feature of her presentation points to a poor prognosis?","choices":["Coexisting obstructive apnea","Significant awake hypoxemia","Her female sex","Her age in the 60s"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Prognosis is poor when there is significant awake hypoxemia at diagnosis and with positive airway pressure therapy. Her awake room-air saturation of 85% is well below normal, and the syndrome already carries high mortality and morbidity, with cardiovascular disease the leading cause of death. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 35, 29, 30"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man has heart failure with reduced ejection fraction and central sleep apnea. His heart failure therapy has been optimized, and continuous positive airway pressure has failed. Which therapy should be avoided because of his heart failure?","choices":["Optimized heart failure therapy","Repeat polysomnography","Adaptive servo-ventilation","Continuous positive airway pressure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Adaptive servo-ventilation is the usual step after continuous positive airway pressure fails, but it is not used for every patient, especially in heart failure with reduced ejection fraction: with poor left ventricular function, reducing preload can decrease cardiac output. Cheyne-Stokes breathing can also be protective. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with a body mass index of 41 kg/m2 has loud snoring and daytime sleepiness. His awake arterial carbon dioxide pressure is 49 mm Hg (normal 35-45). Which sleep study should be avoided in his evaluation?","choices":["Polysomnography with carbon dioxide monitoring","In-laboratory polysomnogram","Pressure titration once diagnosed","Home sleep apnea kit"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In patients with awake hypoventilation a polysomnogram is used, not an at-home kit, and home kits assess obstructive sleep apnea without other sleep disorders. His awake carbon dioxide of 49 mm Hg is awake hypoventilation, and the gold standard for suspected obesity hypoventilation is polysomnography with continuous carbon dioxide monitoring. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 8, 32"},{"q":"A 39-year-old woman with no sleep complaints asks, at a community health fair, to fill out a sleep apnea screening questionnaire to learn whether she has obstructive sleep apnea. What should she be told?","choices":["A high score confirms the disease","It is the routine community screen","It cannot accurately screen her","A low score rules the disease out"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Screening questionnaires should not be routinely used to screen for obstructive sleep apnea in the community, and the questionnaire cannot serve as a true, accurate screening tool in asymptomatic patients. Its value lies in highly symptomatic patients in high-risk settings, such as a preoperative evaluation. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 16, 17, 9"},{"q":"A 56-year-old woman with a body mass index of 44 kg/m2 has daytime hypersomnolence, loud snoring and dyspnea on exertion. Awake on room air, her arterial carbon dioxide pressure is 54 mm Hg (normal 35-45). Her chest radiograph, thyroid function tests, pulmonary function tests and toxicology screen show no other cause of hypoventilation. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Central sleep apnea","Narcolepsy","Obesity hypoventilation syndrome","Obstructive sleep apnea alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Obesity hypoventilation syndrome is a diagnosis of exclusion made when obesity (body mass index above 30) and awake alveolar hypoventilation (arterial carbon dioxide pressure above 45 mm Hg) are present and alternative causes of hypercapnia are excluded. Her 44 kg/m2, 54 mm Hg and negative workup meet all three. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 32, 30, 24, 10"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man with obstructive sleep apnea has a body mass index of 36 kg/m2. He asks what he can do himself, in addition to using his positive airway pressure device. Which risk modification fits him?","choices":["Sleeping on his back each night","A nightly sedative to deepen sleep","An evening glass of wine to relax","Diet and exercise for weight loss"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Obesity heads the risk factors for obstructive sleep apnea, and diet and exercise for weight loss is a named risk modification, together with avoiding alcohol and other sedatives. Bariatric surgery for weight loss may also be helpful. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 6, 12, 13, 15"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with a body mass index of 37 kg/m2 has an awake arterial carbon dioxide pressure of 51 mm Hg (normal 35-45). He has not yet had a chest radiograph, pulmonary function tests, thyroid function tests or a toxicology screen. What must happen before obesity hypoventilation syndrome can be diagnosed?","choices":["Confirm a body mass index above 35","Start bilevel pressure therapy first","Exclude other causes of hypercapnia","Document coexisting obstructive apnea"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Obesity hypoventilation syndrome is a diagnosis of exclusion: obesity (body mass index above 30) and awake hypercapnia (arterial carbon dioxide pressure above 45 mm Hg) must be joined by exclusion of alternative causes of hypercapnia and hypoventilation. The workup includes a chest radiograph, pulmonary function tests, thyroid function tests and a toxicology screen. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 32, 30, 31, 33, 17"},{"q":"A 43-year-old woman with habitual snoring and daytime sleepiness has a home sleep apnea test that does not show obstructive sleep apnea. Her symptoms persist. What is the best next step?","choices":["Reassurance","Repeat home test","Modafinil","Polysomnogram"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. At-home kits will not always diagnose mild obstructive sleep apnea; they are most helpful in moderate to severe disease. A negative home study in a woman with ongoing snoring and sleepiness therefore needs a polysomnogram, which can also find other sleep disorders a home kit cannot assess. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 8, 11"},{"q":"A 50-year-old man has a polysomnogram as part of a research study. He has no sleepiness, fatigue or insomnia, and no one has noted snoring or pauses in his breathing. The study shows 7 predominantly obstructive events per hour. How should this result be interpreted?","choices":["Diagnostic of central sleep apnea","Needs positive airway pressure therapy","Criteria for diagnosis are not met","Diagnostic of obstructive sleep apnea"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Without symptoms, the diagnosis needs 15 or more predominantly obstructive events per hour; 5 or more counts only with a symptom such as sleepiness, fatigue, insomnia, choking or snoring noted by an observer. He has 7 events and no symptoms, so neither criterion is met. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 9, 12, 26"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman with obesity hypoventilation syndrome and obstructive sleep apnea has failed a weight loss program and cannot tolerate positive airway pressure. Which second-line therapy is described as very effective?","choices":["Autotitrating positive airway pressure","Tracheostomy with nocturnal ventilation","Bariatric surgery for weight loss","Adaptive servo-ventilation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. For patients who fail or do not tolerate first-line therapy, second-line options include tracheostomy with nocturnal ventilation, which is very effective, and bariatric surgery, which may be helpful. She has failed weight loss and cannot tolerate positive airway pressure, so she qualifies. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 33, 12, 26"},{"q":"A 51-year-old man is evaluated before elective surgery. He snores loudly and is treated for high blood pressure. He feels rested during the day, and his wife has not seen him stop breathing. His body mass index is 35 kg/m2 and his neck (shirt collar) measures 17 inches. What is his STOP-Bang (snoring, tiredness, observed apnea, pressure, body mass index, age, neck, gender) score?","choices":["Score of 4","Score of 5","Score of 6","Score of 7"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Snoring 1 + tired 0 + observed 0 + treated pressure 1 + body mass index 35 (not more than 35) 0 + age 51 (older than 50) 1 + neck 17 inches 1 + male 1 = 5, the high-risk band (5 to 8). He would also be high risk through 2 yes answers to the first 4 questions plus male gender. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 16, 17"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman who takes long-term opioid medications for chronic pain has frequent awakenings, nonrestorative sleep and daytime sleepiness, with little snoring. Her polysomnogram shows repetitive apneas in the absence of thoracic-abdominal excursion. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Narcolepsy","Central sleep apnea","Obstructive sleep apnea","Obesity hypoventilation syndrome"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Repetitive apneas in the absence of thoracic-abdominal excursion are the polysomnogram finding of central sleep apnea: airflow stops because respiratory effort stops. Opioid medications are a listed risk factor, and frequent awakenings, nonrestorative sleep and sleepiness with little snoring fit. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 24, 25, 26, 10"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man with heart failure has insomnia and frequent awakenings, and central sleep apnea is suspected. Which study establishes the diagnosis?","choices":["Polysomnography","Home sleep apnea test","Arterial blood gas","Thyroid-stimulating hormone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Central sleep apnea is diagnosed by polysomnography, and at-home testing is not sufficient. The study shows repetitive apneas in the absence of thoracic-abdominal excursion, and heart failure is also a reason to use a polysomnogram rather than a home kit. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 26, 8, 32"},{"q":"A 35-year-old woman snores loudly. She feels rested during the day, no one has seen her stop breathing, and she is not treated for high blood pressure. Her body mass index is 36 kg/m2 and her neck (shirt collar) measures 14 inches. Using the STOP-Bang (snoring, tiredness, observed apnea, pressure, body mass index, age, neck, gender) questionnaire, which result fits her?","choices":["Score 1, low risk","Score 2, low risk","Score 2, high risk","Score 3, intermediate risk"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Snoring 1 + tired 0 + observed 0 + pressure 0 + body mass index 36 (more than 35) 1 + age 35 0 + neck 14 inches 0 + female 0 = 2, the low-risk band (0 to 2). The body mass index rule needs 2 or more yes answers to the first 4 questions, and she has 1, so she stays low risk. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 16, 17"},{"q":"A 79-year-old man with untreated obstructive sleep apnea has daytime drowsiness and nocturia. His daughter asks whether treatment is worthwhile at his age. What is accurate?","choices":["Treatment can improve both symptoms","His age makes treatment pointless","His apnea mortality exceeds a younger man's","Nocturia is unrelated to his apnea"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Mortality from obstructive sleep apnea is lower in the elderly because they compensate, but they still have drowsiness and nocturia, and both improve with continuous positive airway pressure. Treating him targets the symptoms that affect his daily life. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 22, 7"},{"q":"A 76-year-old man with moderate obstructive sleep apnea has no teeth and wears full dentures. He cannot tolerate his continuous positive airway pressure mask and asks about a mouthpiece instead. Which option should be avoided?","choices":["Oral appliance","Radiofrequency ablation","Nasal expiratory resistance device","Positional therapy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. An oral appliance is not effective in patients who have no teeth (or in severe obstructive sleep apnea). He is edentulous, so the mouthpiece he asks about is the option to avoid, even though he cannot tolerate his mask. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 12, 13"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man with long-standing untreated obstructive sleep apnea declines therapy. Which cardiovascular effect is expected from his untreated disease?","choices":["Fewer cardiac arrhythmias","Higher risk of aortic dissection","Reduced blood pressure variability","Reduced sympathetic tone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The cardiovascular effects of untreated obstructive sleep apnea include increased sympathetic tone, bradycardia, increased blood pressure variability, increased arrhythmias and an increased risk of aortic aneurysms and aortic dissections. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 21, 18"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with loud snoring, witnessed apneas and daytime sleepiness is seen for evaluation before elective surgery. He has never been tested for sleep apnea. Which property makes the STOP-Bang (snoring, tiredness, observed apnea, pressure, body mass index, age, neck, gender) questionnaire suited to this visit?","choices":["Highest specificity of the questionnaires","It confirms the diagnosis before surgery","It grades his disease severity","Highest sensitivity of the questionnaires"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The questionnaire has been used as a preoperative evaluation tool for people without a known diagnosis of obstructive sleep apnea who may be at risk for perioperative complications, and it has the highest sensitivity among the questionnaires. A highly symptomatic patient in a high-risk setting is where it has value. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 16, 17, 8, 9"},{"q":"A 56-year-old man is seen for evaluation before elective knee surgery and has no known sleep disorder. His wife says he snores loudly enough to be heard through closed doors and has seen him stop breathing during sleep. He denies daytime tiredness. He is treated for high blood pressure. His body mass index is 33 kg/m2 and his neck (shirt collar) measures 16.5 inches. What is his STOP-Bang (snoring, tiredness, observed apnea, pressure, body mass index, age, neck, gender) score?","choices":["Score of 4","Score of 5","Score of 6","Score of 7"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Snoring 1 + tired 0 + observed apnea 1 + treated pressure 1 + body mass index 33 (not more than 35) 0 + age 56 (older than 50) 1 + neck 16.5 inches (16 or larger) 1 + male 1 = 6. A score of 5 to 8 places him in the high-risk group for obstructive sleep apnea before his surgery. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 16, 17"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman has severe obstructive sleep apnea on polysomnogram. She finds her mask uncomfortable and asks whether an oral appliance would work as well. What should she be told?","choices":["It is not effective in severe disease","It is the most effective option","It works if she sleeps on her back","It equals the mask if worn nightly"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. An oral appliance is not effective in severe obstructive sleep apnea or in patients without teeth. Her disease is severe, so the appliance is not a substitute; continuous positive airway pressure remains the most effective treatment for her. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man's polysomnogram shows repeated episodes, each 10 seconds or longer, of slowed, shallow, restricted breathing. Airflow is reduced but does not fall by 90%. How are these episodes classified?","choices":["Obstructive apneas","Central apneas","Mixed apneas","Hypopneas"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A hypopnea is slowed, shallow, restricted breathing that occurs repeatedly during sleep in episodes of 10 seconds or longer. An apnea needs a fall in airflow of 90% or more for at least 10 seconds, which his episodes do not reach, so these are hypopneas. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man had a stroke last year. His wife reports loud snoring and pauses in his breathing during sleep, and a home sleep apnea kit has been ordered. What is the best next step?","choices":["Prescribe modafinil for sleepiness","Change the order to a polysomnogram","Proceed with the home kit as ordered","Start positive airway pressure now"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Stroke is one of the conditions in which a polysomnogram is used instead of an at-home kit. Stroke is also a risk factor for central sleep apnea, and at-home testing assesses obstructive sleep apnea and is not sufficient for central apnea, so the order should change. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 8, 11, 12, 24, 26"},{"q":"A 54-year-old man with a body mass index of 39 kg/m2 has loud snoring and daytime sleepiness. His awake arterial carbon dioxide pressure is 43 mm Hg (normal 35-45), and no other cause of hypoventilation has been found. How do his results measure against the criteria for obesity hypoventilation syndrome?","choices":["Not met: carbon dioxide not above 45","Met: obesity with snoring is enough","Met: his body mass index exceeds 35","Not met: body mass index is too low"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The diagnosis needs all three: obesity (body mass index above 30), awake alveolar hypoventilation with an arterial carbon dioxide pressure above 45 mm Hg, and exclusion of other causes. His body mass index of 39 qualifies, but 43 mm Hg is not above 45, so awake hypoventilation is not shown. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 32, 30, 17"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman with a body mass index of 43 kg/m2, hypersomnolence and a raised serum bicarbonate is being evaluated for obesity hypoventilation syndrome. Which test is the gold standard for her?","choices":["Sleep study with carbon dioxide monitoring","Sleep study with oxygen saturation alone","Home sleep apnea test","Daytime arterial blood gas alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Polysomnography with continuous nocturnal carbon dioxide monitoring is the gold standard for obesity hypoventilation syndrome. Following carbon dioxide through the night captures the hypoventilation of the syndrome alongside the obstructive events that coexist in about 90% of patients. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 32, 31, 30, 8"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man with a body mass index of 47 kg/m2 has facial plethora (facial swelling, puffiness), pedal edema, hepatomegaly and an elevated jugular venous pressure. His serum bicarbonate is 34 mEq/L (normal 22-29) and his awake arterial carbon dioxide pressure is 57 mm Hg (normal 35-45), with alternative causes of hypercapnia excluded. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Central sleep apnea from heart failure","Obstructive sleep apnea alone","Nonalcoholic fatty liver disease","Obesity hypoventilation syndrome"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Many patients with obesity hypoventilation syndrome present late, with right-sided heart failure from pulmonary hypertension (elevated jugular venous pressure, hepatomegaly, pedal edema). Facial plethora and a raised bicarbonate are screening features, and obesity, awake carbon dioxide above 45 mm Hg and excluded alternatives confirm it. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 30, 31, 32, 35, 20"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman's polysomnogram shows events with no airflow. In each, there is no respiratory effort during the first part of the event, and effort resumes during the latter part while airflow is still absent. How are these events classified?","choices":["Mixed apnea","Obstructive apnea","Central apnea","Hypopnea"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A mixed apnea has no airflow, with a lack of respiratory effort during the first part of the event and resumption of effort during the latter part. Her events begin like central apneas and end like obstructive ones, which is exactly the mixed pattern. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"A 78-year-old woman with obstructive sleep apnea has residual daytime sleepiness despite continuous positive airway pressure, and armodafinil is being considered. She has no heart, liver or psychiatric disease. Which approach to its dose fits her?","choices":["The same dose as in younger adults","Avoiding the drug because of age","A lower dose than in younger adults","A higher dose than in younger adults"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Stimulants such as modafinil and armodafinil are given at lower doses in the elderly population. At 78, with no cardiovascular, hepatic or psychiatric condition to add caution, she can be considered for the drug at a reduced dose. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with obstructive sleep apnea has not been helped by continuous positive airway pressure or an oral appliance. He asks which operation would be a cure. Which procedure is described as a cure for obstructive sleep apnea?","choices":["Bariatric surgery","Palatal implants","Radiofrequency ablation","Tracheostomy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Tracheostomy is a cure for obstructive sleep apnea because the site of upper airway obstruction is bypassed. Breathing no longer depends on the narrowed upper airway that collapses during sleep. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man with heart failure has central sleep apnea with Cheyne-Stokes breathing (hyperventilation alternating with apnea) on polysomnography. His leg edema is worsening, his heart failure medicines have not been adjusted in a year, and he takes no opioids. What is the first step?","choices":["Repeat with a home sleep test","Optimize his heart failure therapy","Start adaptive servo-ventilation","Reduce opioid medications"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Central sleep apnea is treated by addressing the underlying cause first, and in heart failure the step is to optimize heart failure therapy. His worsening edema shows his heart failure is not controlled; continuous positive airway pressure is considered afterward, then adaptive servo-ventilation if it fails. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 26, 24"}]};
